### Cline Surface

CLI / Desktop App (shared Core `search_codebase` executor)

### Cline Version

Reproduced from `cline/cline` main at `0809928ab28783c0d2b41c1e56edaf0951dadcab` (`@cline/core` 0.0.89).

### What happened?

When ripgrep is available, `search_codebase` can report a file/line/column without displaying the matching line. Its leading context is also missing, and the next file's leading context can be attached to the previous result.

For two files containing a before line, a line with `needle`, and an after line, searching with `contextLines: 1` produced:

```text
Found 2 results for pattern: needle

beta.txt:2:6
  3: beta after

  1: alpha before


alpha.txt:2:7
  3: alpha after
```

Expected: each result includes its own before line, full matching line marked with `>`, and after line. Neither result should contain text from the other file. With `contextLines: 0`, the matching line should still appear.

The problem is in `sdk/packages/core/src/extensions/tools/executors/search.ts`: `match` events create an empty context array; `context` events are appended to the latest result without checking the file. Ripgrep emits leading context before the match event. The parser also stops as soon as `maxResults` is reached, before reading the last result's trailing context.

### Steps to reproduce

1. From a checkout of the above main commit, run `cd sdk`, `bun install --frozen-lockfile`, and `bun run build:sdk`. Ensure `rg` is on PATH.
2. From `sdk/`, execute the following TypeScript with `bun -e`:

```ts
import { mkdtemp, writeFile, rm } from "node:fs/promises";
import { tmpdir } from "node:os";
import { join } from "node:path";
import { createSearchExecutor } from "./packages/core/src/extensions/tools/executors/search.ts";

const dir = await mkdtemp(join(tmpdir(), "cline-search-repro-"));
try {
  for (const name of ["alpha", "beta"]) {
    await writeFile(join(dir, `${name}.txt`),
      `${name} before\n${name} needle\n${name} after\n`);
  }
  console.log(await createSearchExecutor({ contextLines: 1 })("needle", dir, {
    agentId: "repro", conversationId: "repro", iteration: 1,
  }));
} finally {
  await rm(dir, { recursive: true, force: true });
}
```

3. Check that both matching lines are absent and one file's leading context appears under the other file. Ripgrep may enumerate the files in either order.
4. Repeat with `contextLines: 0`, or `contextLines: 1, maxResults: 1`, to see the missing matching line or incomplete final context.

### Provider/Model

No provider or model is involved. This reproduces directly in the executor with local files and ripgrep.

### System Information

Linux x86_64, Bun 1.4.2, Node.js 26.5.0, ripgrep 13.0.0.

### Related reports

I checked existing issues and PRs. #13771 / #13807 concern the separate per-file `--max-count=1` limit; that PR does not change the JSON match/context assembly described here.

### Cline Surface

Shared SDK agent runtime. The affected path is `AgentRuntime` in `@cline/agents`, which can be used by Cline clients and SDK integrations. This report reproduces the problem directly at that boundary rather than attributing it to a particular extension or desktop UI.

### Cline Version

Source checkout at `b006326612c78fb1acad5e64aa305165c778141c`; `@cline/agents` version `0.0.92`.

### What happened?

Cancelling a run does not prevent the runtime from dispatching tools that have not started yet. With sequential tool execution, a model can return several tool calls in one response. If the first operation is still running when the caller invokes `runtime.abort()`, the runtime waits for that operation to settle and then starts the next call with an already-aborted signal. A tool that does not independently inspect that signal can still perform its operation.

A concrete example is a response containing a finite waiting operation followed by an editor call. The caller stops the run while the first operation is in progress, expecting the queued edit to be cancelled. When the first operation finishes, the editor is nevertheless invoked and changes the file. The ordinary run can ultimately report `aborted`, so that status does not establish that pending operations were prevented from starting. This makes Stop unreliable at the point where users most need it: before the next operation changes their workspace.

The distinction between an operation already running and an operation merely present in the model response matters here. A running tool might need to finish or cooperate with cancellation. The editor in this reproduction has not started when the cancellation is requested. The runtime owns the decision to dispatch it and should decline to do so.

There is also a status edge case in the same execution path. If an operation that was already running completes successfully and has `lifecycle.completesRun`, terminal-tool handling can finish the run as `completed` after cancellation. Cancellation should take precedence once the batch results have been safely recorded.

### Steps to reproduce

The reproduction uses a scripted model and the actual built-in editor executor, so it does not depend on model sampling, credentials, a network connection, or a timer race. The two promises determine exactly when the first tool starts and when it may finish.

From the repository root, install and build with the supported toolchain:

```sh
bun install --frozen-lockfile
bun run build:sdk
```

Save the following as `repro-stop.ts` in the repository root and run `bun run repro-stop.ts`. It creates a file in a temporary directory rather than editing repository files.

```ts
import { mkdtemp, readFile, writeFile } from "node:fs/promises";
import { tmpdir } from "node:os";
import { join } from "node:path";
import { AgentRuntime } from "./sdk/packages/agents/src/agent-runtime.ts";
import { createEditorExecutor } from "./sdk/packages/core/src/extensions/tools/executors/editor.ts";

const cwd = await mkdtemp(join(tmpdir(), "cline-stop-"));
const file = join(cwd, "user-work.txt");
await writeFile(file, "USER ORIGINAL");
const started = Promise.withResolvers<void>();
const finish = Promise.withResolvers<void>();
const editor = createEditorExecutor();
let editorInvoked = false;

const runtime = new AgentRuntime({
  model: {
    async *stream() {
      yield { type: "tool-call-delta", toolCallId: "wait", toolName: "wait", input: {} };
      yield {
        type: "tool-call-delta", toolCallId: "edit", toolName: "editor",
        input: { path: file, old_text: "USER ORIGINAL", new_text: "CHANGED AFTER STOP" },
      };
      yield { type: "finish", reason: "tool-calls" };
    },
  },
  toolExecution: "sequential",
  tools: [
    {
      name: "wait", description: "Controlled finite operation",
      inputSchema: { type: "object" },
      async execute() {
        started.resolve();
        await finish.promise;
        return "finished";
      },
    },
    {
      name: "editor", description: "Built-in editor",
      inputSchema: { type: "object" },
      async execute(input, context) {
        editorInvoked = true;
        return editor(input, cwd, context);
      },
    },
  ],
});

const run = runtime.run("Do two operations");
await started.promise;
runtime.abort("User pressed Stop");
finish.resolve();
const result = await run;
console.log({ status: result.status, editorInvoked, contents: await readFile(file, "utf8") });
```

The current execution path invokes the editor and changes the contents to `CHANGED AFTER STOP`. The expected result is `status: "aborted"`, `editorInvoked: false`, and unchanged contents, `USER ORIGINAL`.

### Expected behavior and conversation continuity

After cancellation, the runtime should allow already-started work to settle according to the existing tool contract, preserve its completed result, and prevent remaining calls from entering their executors. That rule should apply both to subsequent sequential calls and to a subsequent group of tools opted into parallel execution.

Cancellation also needs to leave a usable conversation. The assistant message already contains the entire batch of tool calls, including those that will now be skipped. Each needs a corresponding result recording that it was cancelled. Dropping the pending calls' results or discarding the successful result of the first operation would leave incomplete history for a later `continue()` request. No next model turn should run until the caller explicitly continues the conversation.

### Where the cancellation boundary is missing

In `sdk/packages/agents/src/agent-runtime.ts`, `executeToolCalls()` prepares the batch, then executes sequential calls or adjacent parallel groups through `executePreparedTool()`. The latter emits `tool-started` and awaits its event handlers before invoking `prepared.tool.execute()`. Neither arriving at this method after an earlier tool finishes nor resuming after that awaited event establishes that the abort signal is still clear.

Passing `context.signal` to the executor is useful for operations that are already running, but it does not replace the runtime's pre-dispatch decision. The built-in editor illustrates this clearly because its executor performs filesystem editing without checking the context signal.

Batch results are appended to the conversation only after `executeToolCalls()` returns. The cancellation boundary therefore needs to preserve the batch's completed results and supply cancellation results for unstarted calls before exiting the run. A second check is needed after recording those results, before the runtime can select a completing tool or continue its loop.

This execution boundary is separate from the preparation and manual-approval checks proposed in [#13892](https://github.com/cline/cline/pull/13892). Preventing preparation after cancellation does not cover cancellation that occurs while the first executable tool is running, or while a `tool-started` handler is being awaited.

### Provider/Model

Deterministic scripted SDK model. The supplied tool-call batch isolates runtime behavior after a model response has already been received: cancellation and dispatch are controlled by the SDK rather than by provider sampling.

### Diagnostics and system information

Reproduced on Windows with Node `24.11.1` and Bun `1.4.2`. The fix has been checked with the complete SDK build, the Agents package's 154 tests, its TypeScript check, and Biome for both affected files. This report concerns pending tool dispatch and preservation of run history; termination of external processes that have already started is outside its scope.

### Linked fix

The implementation is available in [PR #14982](https://github.com/cline/cline/pull/14982). It addresses this report as a separate, focused change.

### Cline Surface

VSCode Extension

### Cline Version

4.1.24 (Next / SDK bundle). Also reproduced on `main` at a42df8c (2026-10-16); 4.1.25 has no changes in this area.

### Beta version

- [ ] I am using a beta version of Cline

### What happened?

With the **Anthropic** provider (direct API key), the request Cline sends to `/v1/messages` contains exactly **one** cache breakpoint: on the last text block of the last `user`-role message. During an agent loop the conversation grows as `assistant (tool_use)` → `tool (tool_result)` → …, and no new `user` message is added. The breakpoint therefore stays on the original prompt for the whole loop. Every assistant/tool turn after it is billed as uncached input on every request, and it is never written to the cache.

This is the same mechanism as the Bedrock report in #13738. #14481 fixes only the Bedrock path; its description notes that the non-Bedrock `anthropic-cache-control` path "is also left as is".

**Expected:** each request in a tool loop reads the previous prefix from cache and writes only the new suffix, as the 4.0.x Anthropic handler did (it marked the system prompt plus the last two user messages, and tool results were user-role there).

**Request body captured from Cline's own pipeline** (`createGateway` with a mocked `fetch`, Anthropic provider, after two tool rounds):

```
top-level cache_control: none
system: no cache_control / tools: no cache_control
[0] user: text  <- cache_control (the only breakpoint)
[1] assistant: text | tool_use
[2] user: tool_result
[3] assistant: text | tool_use
[4] user: tool_result
```

**Root cause (two parts):**

1. **Breakpoint search ignores tool results.** [`buildAiSdkRequestMessages`](https://github.com/cline/cline/blob/a42df8c8d5fd9d9f9a9073589baffe32ea9bb2f0/sdk/packages/llms/src/providers/ai-sdk.ts#L428) only marks the last message with `role === "user"`. Tool results are pushed as `role: "tool"` by [`AgentRuntime`](https://github.com/cline/cline/blob/a42df8c8d5fd9d9f9a9073589baffe32ea9bb2f0/sdk/packages/agents/src/agent-runtime.ts#L2628) and kept that way by [`ai-sdk-format.ts`](https://github.com/cline/cline/blob/a42df8c8d5fd9d9f9a9073589baffe32ea9bb2f0/sdk/packages/shared/src/llms/ai-sdk-format.ts#L868), so they are never chosen.
2. **The request-level automatic-caching option is silently dropped.** [`buildAnthropicProviderOptions`](https://github.com/cline/cline/blob/a42df8c8d5fd9d9f9a9073589baffe32ea9bb2f0/sdk/packages/llms/src/providers/routing/anthropic-compatible.ts#L399) puts `cache_control` (snake_case, from [`createEphemeralCacheControl`](https://github.com/cline/cline/blob/a42df8c8d5fd9d9f9a9073589baffe32ea9bb2f0/sdk/packages/llms/src/providers/routing/utils.ts#L14)) into `providerOptions.anthropic`. `@ai-sdk/anthropic` 4.0.67 parses those options with a non-passthrough zod schema and only maps the camelCase `cacheControl` to the top-level `cache_control`. The snake_case key is removed, so Anthropic's automatic caching (which would advance the breakpoint by itself) never turns on. The recorded cassette `src/tests/provider-vcr/anthropic-claude-sonnet-4-6.json` shows the same: no top-level `cache_control`.

Existing tests did not catch this. Three `gateway.test.ts` cases assert the snake_case key on the options passed to `streamText` (the AI SDK input), not on the wire body. One case (`formats assistant tool calls and tool results…`) asserts that the marker stays on the user message in a tool continuation.

**Impact:** this grows with loop length and gets worse on models with cheap cache reads. Rough model, not a billing measurement: with a ~7k prefix and ~2.5k tokens per step, a 25-step loop is about 18% cache reads instead of about 93%, and roughly 6× the input cost. This may also explain #13117 (token use roughly doubled from 4.0.12 to 4.1.x; closed for lack of detail).

**Possible fixes** (I checked both locally on `main` with the test below):

- **A. Send top-level automatic caching.** Emit `{ cacheControl: { type: "ephemeral" } }` (camelCase) from `buildAnthropicProviderOptions`. The wire body then carries a top-level `cache_control` and Anthropic advances the breakpoint itself. The new wire test passes; the 3 gateway tests that assert the snake_case shape need updating. Caveat: some Anthropic-compatible custom base URLs may not accept the top-level field, so this may need to be limited to `api.anthropic.com`, or combined with B.
- **B. Advance the explicit marker, mirroring #14481.** When the newest cacheable message is a `tool` message, put the cache provider options on its last `tool-result` part. `@ai-sdk/anthropic` then sets `cache_control` on that `tool_result` block. This is about 20 lines in `buildAiSdkRequestMessages`. The new wire test passes; 1 existing gateway test asserts the old placement.

<details><summary>Diff for option B</summary>

```diff
--- a/sdk/packages/llms/src/providers/ai-sdk.ts
+++ b/sdk/packages/llms/src/providers/ai-sdk.ts
@@ import {
 	applyPromptCacheToLastTextPart,
+	createPromptCacheProviderOptions,
 	shouldApplyPromptCache,
 } from "./routing/anthropic-compatible";
@@ function buildAiSdkRequestMessages(
 	for (let i = aiMessages.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
-		if (aiMessages[i]?.role === "user") {
+		const message = aiMessages[i];
+		// Tool results are emitted as role "tool". Without this branch the
+		// breakpoint stays on the last human message for the whole agent loop.
+		if (includeAnthropic && message?.role === "tool" && Array.isArray(message.content)) {
+			const parts = message.content as Array<Record<string, unknown>>;
+			const lastResult = parts.findLastIndex((part) => part?.type === "tool-result");
+			if (lastResult >= 0) {
+				parts[lastResult] = {
+					...parts[lastResult],
+					providerOptions: createPromptCacheProviderOptions(
+						request.providerId,
+						includeAnthropic,
+					),
+				};
+				break;
+			}
+		}
+		if (message?.role === "user") {
 			applyPromptCacheToLastTextPart(
-				aiMessages[i],
+				message,
 				request.providerId,
 				includeAnthropic,
 			);
```

</details>

### Steps to reproduce

1. Save the test below as `sdk/packages/llms/src/providers/routing/anthropic-prompt-cache.wire.test.ts`. It sends no network traffic and uses a dummy key.
2. In `sdk/packages/llms`, run `bunx vitest run src/providers/routing/anthropic-prompt-cache.wire.test.ts`.
3. On `main`: **1 failed, 2 passed**. The tool-continuation case fails because the final `tool_result` block has no cache breakpoint and there is no top-level `cache_control`. With fix A or fix B, all 3 pass.

<details><summary>Test file</summary>

```ts
import type { AgentMessage } from "@cline/shared";
import { describe, expect, it, vi } from "vitest";
import { createGateway } from "../gateway";

type AnthropicBlock = Record<string, unknown> & { type: string };
type AnthropicMessage = { role: string; content: AnthropicBlock[] };

async function captureAnthropicRequest(
	messages: AgentMessage[],
	modelId = "claude-sonnet-4-5",
): Promise<Record<string, unknown>> {
	let requestBody: Record<string, unknown> | undefined;
	const fetchMock = vi.fn(
		async (
			_input: Parameters<typeof fetch>[0],
			init?: Parameters<typeof fetch>[1],
		) => {
			requestBody = JSON.parse(String(init?.body)) as Record<string, unknown>;
			return new Response(
				JSON.stringify({
					type: "error",
					error: { type: "invalid_request_error", message: "captured" },
				}),
				{ status: 400, headers: { "content-type": "application/json" } },
			);
		},
	);
	const gateway = createGateway({
		providerConfigs: [
			{ providerId: "anthropic", apiKey: "test", fetch: fetchMock as typeof fetch },
		],
	});
	const stream = await gateway.stream({
		providerId: "anthropic",
		modelId,
		systemPrompt: "You are Cline.",
		messages,
		tools: [
			{
				name: "read_files",
				description: "Read files",
				inputSchema: {
					type: "object",
					properties: { paths: { type: "array", items: { type: "string" } } },
					required: ["paths"],
				},
			},
		],
	});
	try {
		for await (const _event of stream) {
			// Drain the stream so the mocked request is sent.
		}
	} catch {
		// The controlled 400 response is expected after request capture.
	}
	expect(fetchMock).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(1);
	if (!requestBody) {
		throw new Error("Expected the mocked fetch to capture a request body");
	}
	return requestBody;
}

/** True when the request's final content block is covered by a cache breakpoint. */
function lastBlockIsCached(body: Record<string, unknown>): boolean {
	if (body.cache_control) {
		// Top-level automatic caching: Anthropic places the breakpoint on the
		// last cacheable block and advances it as the conversation grows.
		return true;
	}
	const messages = body.messages as AnthropicMessage[];
	const lastBlock = messages.at(-1)?.content.at(-1);
	return Boolean(lastBlock?.cache_control);
}

const createdAt = 1;
const userPrompt: AgentMessage = {
	id: "user-1",
	role: "user",
	content: [{ type: "text", text: "Fix the bug" }],
	createdAt,
};
function toolRound(n: number): AgentMessage[] {
	return [
		{
			id: `assistant-${n}`,
			role: "assistant",
			content: [
				{
					type: "tool-call",
					toolCallId: `call-${n}`,
					toolName: "read_files",
					input: { paths: [`file-${n}.ts`] },
				},
			],
			createdAt,
		},
		{
			id: `tool-${n}`,
			role: "tool",
			content: [
				{
					type: "tool-result",
					toolCallId: `call-${n}`,
					toolName: "read_files",
					output: `contents of file-${n}.ts`,
				},
			],
			createdAt,
		},
	];
}

describe("anthropic prompt-cache wire format", () => {
	it("caches the opening user prompt", async () => {
		const body = await captureAnthropicRequest([userPrompt]);
		expect(lastBlockIsCached(body)).toBe(true);
	});

	it("advances the cache breakpoint through tool-result continuations", async () => {
		const body = await captureAnthropicRequest([
			userPrompt,
			...toolRound(1),
			...toolRound(2),
		]);
		// On main the only breakpoint stays on messages[0] (the original user
		// prompt), so every assistant/tool turn after it is re-sent uncached
		// on every request of the agent loop.
		expect(lastBlockIsCached(body)).toBe(true);
	});

	it("caches a new human turn after a tool round", async () => {
		const body = await captureAnthropicRequest([
			userPrompt,
			...toolRound(1),
			{
				id: "assistant-done",
				role: "assistant",
				content: [{ type: "text", text: "Done." }],
				createdAt,
			},
			{
				id: "user-2",
				role: "user",
				content: [{ type: "text", text: "Now add a test" }],
				createdAt,
			},
		]);
		expect(lastBlockIsCached(body)).toBe(true);
	});
});
```

</details>

### Provider/Model

anthropic:claude-opus-5-5 (direct API key). The test above uses claude-sonnet-4-5 and fails the same way; the behavior does not depend on the model.

### Diagnostics

Next (SDK) bundle. The bug is in request construction in `@cline/llms`, so it does not depend on the IDE build.

### System Information

OS: macOS (Apple Silicon)

### Cline Surface

CLI

### Cline Version

CLI 3.0.73 (`main` at `1619168`)

### Beta version

- [ ] I am using a beta version of Cline

### What happened?

The interactive MCP wizard (`apps/cli/src/wizards/mcp/index.ts`) splits the stdio command with `parseStdioCommand` and stores `parts[0]` as `command` and the rest as `args`.

`parseStdioCommand` treats every backslash as an escape character, inside double quotes and outside quotes alike, and drops it:

```ts
if (char === "\\") {
  escaping = true;
  continue;
}
```

On Windows, every path the user types loses its separators. A server added with `node C:\Users\me\mcp\server.js` is saved with the argument `C:Usersmemcpserver.js`, so the server fails to start because the file doesn't exist. Quoting the path, as most users do for `Program Files`, does not help, because escapes are processed inside double quotes too.

### Possible Solution

1. Treat a backslash as an escape only when it comes before a quote character, or before whitespace outside quotes (`my\ dir`).
2. Keep any other backslash literally, and never process escapes inside single quotes.
3. Add Windows-path cases next to the existing `parses quoted stdio command arguments` test in `settings.test.ts`.

```ts
export function parseStdioCommand(input: string): string[] {
  const tokens: string[] = [];
  let current = "";
  let quote: '"' | "'" | undefined;
  const chars = [...input.trim()];
  for (let i = 0; i < chars.length; i++) {
    const char = chars[i]!;
    // A backslash escapes only a quote or whitespace, so Windows paths such as
    // C:\Users\me\server.js keep their separators.
    if (char === "\\" && quote !== "'") {
      const next = chars[i + 1];
      if (next !== undefined && (next === '"' || next === "'" || (!quote && /\s/.test(next)))) {
        current += next;
        i++;
        continue;
      }
      current += char;
      continue;
    }
    if (quote) {
      if (char === quote) quote = undefined;
      else current += char;
      continue;
    }
    if (char === '"' || char === "'") { quote = char; continue; }
    if (/\s/.test(char)) {
      if (current) { tokens.push(current); current = ""; }
      continue;
    }
    current += char;
  }
  if (current) tokens.push(current);
  return tokens;
}
```

With this version:

- Both commands above keep their backslashes.
- `node my\ dir/server.js --name "a \"b\""` still gives `["node","my dir/server.js","--name","a \"b\""]`.
- The existing `npx -y "@scope/server name" --root "my dir"` case still gives the same result.

I'm happy to send a PR.

### Steps to reproduce

Call the parser the wizard uses:

```ts
parseStdioCommand(String.raw`node C:\Users\me\mcp\server.js`)
// ["node","C:Usersmemcpserver.js"]

parseStdioCommand(String.raw`"C:\Program Files\nodejs\node.exe" C:\tools\server.js`)
// ["C:Program Filesnodejsnode.exe","C:toolsserver.js"]
```

The same happens through `cline mcp` â†’ add server â†’ stdio, entering either command above.

Expected:

```
["node","C:\\Users\\me\\mcp\\server.js"]
["C:\\Program Files\\nodejs\\node.exe","C:\\tools\\server.js"]
```

### Provider/Model

Not applicable.

### Diagnostics

Reproduced by running `parseStdioCommand` from source with `npx tsx`.

### System Information

Linux 6.12 (Debian 13), Node.js 20.19.2. The parser is platform-independent, but the problem affects Windows paths.

